    "## Baseline Models\n",
    "\n",
    "### Train, validation, and test split\n",
    "\n",
    "We shuffled the ratings using `random_state=42` and initially split them into 80% training, 10% validation, and 10% test data.\n",
    "\n",
    "To ensure that every user and movie in validation and test appeared in training, we moved 13 ratings from validation and 18 ratings from test into training.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Split | Number of ratings |\n",
    "|---|---:|\n",
    "| Training | 80,031 |\n",
    "| Validation | 9,987 |\n",
    "| Test | 9,982 |\n",
    "\n",
    "This produces a **warm-start evaluation**. It avoids unseen users and movies in the evaluation sets, but does not measure performance on genuinely new users or movies.\n",
    "\n",
    "All model parameters were calculated using training data only. Validation data were used to compare models and select the SVD rank. The test set was reserved for final evaluation.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Mean-based baselines\n",
    "\n",
    "We implemented three simple prediction models:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Global mean:** predicts the average of all training ratings.\n",
    "- **User mean:** predicts the user’s average training rating.\n",
    "- **Item mean:** predicts the movie’s average training rating.\n",
    "\n",
    "We evaluated predictions using RMSE and MAE. Lower values indicate better rating prediction.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model | Validation RMSE | Validation MAE |\n",
    "|---|---:|---:|\n",
    "| Global mean | 1.1244 | 0.9433 |\n",
    "| User mean | 1.0358 | 0.8290 |\n",
    "| Item mean | **1.0223** | **0.8144** |\n",
    "\n",
    "The item-mean model performed best among these three baselines.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Naive zero-filled truncated SVD\n",
    "\n",
    "We constructed a training rating matrix with 943 rows and 1,682 columns. Observed training ratings were inserted into the matrix, and all remaining entries were filled with zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "We computed its singular value decomposition:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "R_0 = U\\Sigma V^T\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For each candidate rank \\(k\\), we retained the first \\(k\\) singular components and reconstructed an approximate rating matrix:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\widehat{R}_k = U_k\\Sigma_kV_k^T\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Predictions were read from this matrix at the user–movie positions in the validation set. Errors were calculated only against actual validation ratings, not against the inserted zeros. Predictions were neither rounded nor clipped to the rating range.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Rank \\(k\\) | Validation RMSE | Validation MAE |\n",
    "|---|---:|---:|\n",
    "| 1 | 2.9477 | 2.7004 |\n",
    "| 5 | 2.6976 | 2.4346 |\n",
    "| 10 | **2.6165** | **2.3469** |\n",
    "| 20 | 2.6457 | 2.3771 |\n",
    "| 50 | 2.8864 | 2.6165 |\n",
    "| 100 | 3.1835 | 2.9286 |\n",
    "\n",
    "Among the tested ranks, **\\(k=10\\)** achieved the lowest validation RMSE. However, it performed substantially worse than all three mean-based baselines.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "Only approximately 5% of the training matrix contains observed ratings. Zero-filling therefore introduces many artificial zeros.\n",
    "\n",
    "Truncated SVD approximates the entire zero-filled matrix, treating these zeros as numerical targets. However, an unknown rating is not a genuine rating of zero. This can pull predictions downward and helps explain the poor validation performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "Increasing the rank did not consistently improve prediction accuracy. A larger rank reconstructs the zero-filled input more closely, but that does not necessarily improve predictions for held-out ratings.\n",
    "\n",
    "These results provide a baseline for comparison with a factorization method that fits only observed training ratings. Final test-set results will be reported after model selection is complete."
